A start tag whose attributes span lines opens a continuation region a single-line tag never
opens, and each element family names that region for itself. These carry one element of each.

<div
  class="a"
  id="b">the structure family</div>

<abbr
  class="a">the inline family</abbr>

<object
  data="a">the object family</object>

<title
  class="a">the metadata family</title>

<madeuptag
  class="a">an element no family names</madeuptag>

<svg><metadata
  class="a">svg metadata</metadata><tspan
  class="a">svg inline</tspan><altGlyph
  class="a">the svg other family</altGlyph><circle
  cx="1"
  cy="2">svg object</circle><g
  class="a">svg structure</g></svg>

<math><mi
  class="a">math inline</mi><mrow
  class="a">math structure</mrow><mglyph
  class="a">math object</mglyph></math>

A blank line ends a start tag here, where TiddlyWiki carries it: the parser skips whitespace
across one while attributes keep parsing, so its own core writes tags this way. Counted across
2583 files, six accepted start tags carry a blank line outside a quoted value and every one of
them wears a widget name; no plain HTML tag anywhere carries one. So the bound costs six real
constructs in a single core file and closes a region that otherwise takes the rest of the file.

<$set

  name="targetTiddler"
  value=<<currentTiddler>>

>the widget still holds its attributes</$set>

A table caption stands above its rows.

|A caption|c
|!head|!two|h
|body|row|
|foot|two|f
